# /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#
# The privileged commands the non-root `boat` service user may run. The daemon
# (systemd/boat.service) runs as `boat`; the individual host mutations need root,
# the daemon does not. That gap is this file, and it is the single biggest
# blast-radius reduction in the Atlas -> Boat split (spec/33-boat.md §12).
#
# Modelled on Atlas's scripts/sudoers.d/atlas-tunnel: every command is enumerated
# with its arguments. There is no wildcard shell, no ALL, and no bare
# /usr/bin/systemctl — a line that grants an unconstrained shell or an
# unconstrained systemctl grants root, and then running the daemon non-root buys
# nothing at all.
#
# Install (operator step; Boat never edits sudoers):
#   sudo useradd --system --home-dir /var/lib/boat --shell /usr/sbin/nologin boat
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root sudoers.d/boat /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#
# Paths below are Ubuntu with merged /usr, resolved on a real host. Adjust per
# distro, and resolve rather than guess — sudoers matches the resolved path, so a
# binary in the other directory is a silent denial:
#   command -v systemctl test install mkdir rm rmdir dmsetup sh cat touch stat \
#       chown chmod mknod mount umount mktemp mv grep jq ss ip nft lvs lvcreate \
#       lvchange lvextend lvremove vgmknodes udevadm blockdev tune2fs e2fsck \
#       ssh-keygen
#
# On wildcards: in a sudoers command PATH a `*` does not match `/`, but in a
# command ARGUMENT it does — and it matches SPACES too. sudo joins the user's
# arguments into one string before matching, so a `*` that spans a space is a `*`
# that has grown the command a second operand, and no amount of care about the
# rest of the line undoes that.
#
# THE RULE: a `*` is pinned by the literal text that follows it ONLY when the
# attacker cannot supply that literal himself. Where the literal does no work, the
# field is spelled out character by character instead. Four ways the literal does
# no work, every one found by probing a real host rather than by reading:
#
#   - `firecracker-vm@*.service`. Every unit on the host ends in `.service`, so
#     the suffix pins nothing: `systemctl stop firecracker-vm@x.service
#     sshd.service` satisfied it. Combined with the drop-in lines below it was a
#     path to root, and both families now carry the spelled-out UUID.
#   - a `*` inside an `sh -c` script. A `#` in the wildcard comments out the whole
#     literal tail the pattern is anchored on, so the tail matches the pattern and
#     never reaches the shell: `cd /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/x; cmd;
#     #/jail/...` satisfied the SendCtrlAltDel line. Every `sh -c` line here
#     therefore has every variable field spelled out.
#   - a `*` with nothing after it at all, on a command that destroys: `rm -rf` of
#     a VM tree, `lvremove` of a volume.
#   - a `*` that stands for a UUID path segment, EVEN with a literal after it,
#     because the attacker appends that literal to his own last operand. `cat
#     .../*/network.env` with `*`=`x /etc/shadow x` renders `cat .../x /etc/shadow
#     x/network.env` and reads /etc/shadow; `test -d .../*` is an `-r/-w/-e` oracle
#     the same way. The trailing `/network.env` looked load-bearing and was not, so
#     every read and marker line that names a VM by `*` now spells its UUID out
#     too — the reads are no longer trusted to be harmless just because they read.
#
# So the forms are:
#
#   `*`           a field with NO fixed shape and a genuinely load-bearing literal
#                 after it, or a value a code-side check has already validated
#                 (an address netip parsed, a numeric jq argument). The comment on
#                 the line says which, and what the residual is.
#   `?`           a field whose length is fixed by construction (the eight hex
#                 digits of a hostname suffix).
#   `[0-9a-f]`    the 8-4-4-4-12 UUID, spelled out. A class matches neither a
#                 space nor a `/`, so the field cannot grow an operand and cannot
#                 walk out of its directory. This is now the default for a UUID
#                 wherever one appears — read, marker, write or destroy alike.
#   `[0-9A-Za-z]` the six characters mktemp generates, which is what anchors the
#                 whole rebuild identity block.
#
# The spelled-out forms are ugly, and they are ugly on purpose. This file is the
# privilege boundary; a boundary that is pleasant to read and does not hold is
# worth less than one that holds.
#
# Boat validates the UUID at the API boundary before any of these are rendered —
# sudoers is the second line of defence, not the first. That first line is now
# real: internal/api refuses a request whose {uuid} is not the 8-4-4-4-12 shape
# (api.refuseMalformedUUID), and internal/park validates a directory name learned
# from the host before it becomes a path or an nft identifier, the way the
# adoption scan already did. It used to be a claim with no caller — paths.IsUUID
# existed and nothing on an HTTP path called it — which is exactly why the UUID is
# ALSO spelled out here: the boundary and the allow-list each hold on their own.
#
# Scope is every verb internal/vm and internal/park render today: start, stop,
# observe, pause, resume, sleep, wake, resize, rebuild, terminate, adopt, and the
# wake trap. Each later work order adds its own verbs' commands here explicitly.
# Widening an existing line to cover a new verb is not a smaller change than
# adding a line; it is an unreviewable one.
#
# Every line below was checked against a real host with `sudo -u boat sudo -n -l
# <the exact rendered command>`, not by reading. Three separate bugs in this file
# — a `-f` where the code called `-S`, `/usr/sbin/ss` where the binary is in
# /usr/bin, and a Firecracker API line whose quoting had drifted — each shipped
# past a fully green test suite, because the tests stub above the layer that
# renders these strings. Reading is not verification here.

# --- Lifecycle: the per-VM unit, and only the per-VM unit -------------------
# Instance-scoped to one firecracker-vm@ instance, so this grants nothing over
# sshd, over boat.service itself, or over any other unit on the host.
#
# The instance is the spelled-out UUID and not `firecracker-vm@*.service`, and
# that is not fussiness. `.service` pins nothing, because every unit name ends in
# it: `systemctl stop firecracker-vm@x.service sshd.service` satisfied the
# wildcard form, and so did `systemctl start firecracker-vm@x.service
# <anything>.service`. A daemon that could render one argument containing a space
# — which is all it takes, since sudo joins the arguments before matching — could
# stop sshd or start any unit on the host through this alias. The spelled-out
# form cannot match a space, so the instance is exactly one VM's.
#
# Only the commands the Go actually renders WITH `sudo`. `is-active --quiet` and
# `systemctl show` are rendered unprivileged, so grants for them were dead — and
# the `show -p *` one was worse than dead: `*` matches spaces, so
# `show -p MainPID sshd.service firecracker-vm@x.service` satisfied it and the
# rule reached units this alias promises never to touch. A grant nothing exercises
# is a grant nobody notices is wrong.
#
# `is-active` is read-only and would work unprivileged too; it stays because the
# port carries the Python's `sudo` prefix through and granting a read that
# changes nothing costs nothing.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_UNIT = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start firecracker-vm@[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop firecracker-vm@[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed firecracker-vm@[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-active firecracker-vm@[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].service

# --- Observation: the on-disk markers --------------------------------------
# The VM tree is 0700-owned by the per-VM uid, so even a stat needs root. All
# four are `test`: they return a boolean and change nothing.
#
# The UUID is spelled out even though these only read, and that is the correction
# of a belief this block used to hold: that "it only reads" made a trailing `*`
# safe. It did not. `test -d /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*` ends in the
# wildcard, so `*` matched `x -o -r /etc/shadow` and the line became an arbitrary
# `-r/-w/-e` oracle on any path; the `*/sleeping` and `*/…/READY` lines were the
# same trick with the literal tail appended to the attacker's own last operand. A
# read that an attacker steers is not harmless, and a `test` is a filesystem
# oracle whether or not it writes. A character class matches neither a space nor a
# `/`, so each field is now exactly one VM's.
#
# The jail root is a SECOND, deeper probe and needs its own line, because a `*`
# in a sudoers argument does not match `/` — so the VM-directory grant above
# authorises nothing below it. Without this the coherence check reads every
# healthy VM as "VM directory has no jail tree" and quarantines the lot, which
# is the same host-empty answer the listing bug produced, one layer down.
#
# The socket check is `test -S`, not `-f`. sudo matches arguments literally, so
# the flag is part of the grant: a `-f` line does not authorize the `-S` call the
# code makes, and `-f` is false for a socket in any case. The two together
# produced a rule that denied precisely the command it existed for.
# network.env's PRESENCE probe is here because a Go change needed it and did not
# bring it. internal/park used to read that sidecar with RunUnchecked and take any
# failure as "this VM has no address", so a denied read became a green sleep with
# no trap armed. The fix probes first — and a probe is `test -f`, which the `cat`
# grant does not cover, because sudo matches the COMMAND and not the file. So the
# stricter code hard-failed terminate, sleep and wake on every host until this
# line existed. Fourth instance of one shape in a day: a call site tightened, the
# allow-list left behind. It is the argument for a test that renders every sudo
# template in internal/ and checks each against this file.
#
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_MARKERS = \
    /usr/bin/test -d /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f], \
    /usr/bin/test -d /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]/jail/firecracker/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]/root, \
