# --- Adoption: reading what the host already holds -------------------------
# Every one of these reads and none of them writes. Adoption runs on a host that
# may already be serving VMs, so a scan that could mutate would be a scan nobody
# could safely run. But a read is not thereby safe, and the `cat` below is why
# the UUID is spelled out and not left a `*`: `cat .../*/network.env` with
# `*`=`x /etc/shadow x` renders `cat .../x /etc/shadow x/network.env`, and cat
# reads /etc/shadow along the way — the wildcard grew a second file operand and
# the literal `/network.env` was satisfied by appending it to the attacker's own
# last operand. "Reads only" bounds nothing when the read target is a wildcard.
# The character class matches neither a space nor a `/`, so the cat names exactly
# one VM's sidecar.
#
# `network.env` is read rather than re-derived: it is the sidecar the systemd
# hooks themselves read to rebuild a VM's networking after a reboot, so it is the
# host's own record of which tap and namespace belong to which VM. Re-deriving
# those names risks drifting from the writer, and a drift here means adopting a
# VM holding another VM's tap.
#
# The namespace read is `ip -n`, NEVER `ip netns exec`. `ip netns exec <ns> <cmd>`
# runs <cmd> as root, and a network namespace isolates networking only — the
# command keeps full root access to the real filesystem. No sudoers pattern can
# constrain that form, because the wildcard standing in for the namespace name
# also matches a command: `ip netns exec <ns> sh -c '...' ip link show` satisfies
# any rule written around it. `ip -n` takes the namespace as a flag and can only
# ever re-execute ip, so the wildcard has nothing to reach.
#
# The listing and its presence probe are the entry point of the whole scan, and
# they need root for a reason worth stating: /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines is
# 0700 and root-owned (bootstrap-server.py), so an unprivileged `ls` fails with
# EACCES on every bootstrapped host. Boat read that failure as "this host holds
# no VMs" and quarantined every VM its units and volumes named. A host serving
# VMs reported that it held none — the one answer a control plane must never be
# handed by accident. The scan now proves absence with `test -d` and treats a
# failed listing as a failed scan, so both lines are needed and both are fully
# literal.
#
# The wake trap's boot sweep runs the same listing, and was dark the same way:
# after a reboot nftables is empty and every sleeping VM's unit is suppressed by
# its own marker, so that sweep is the only thing that re-parks them.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_ADOPTION = \
    /usr/bin/test -d /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines, \
    /usr/bin/ls -1 /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines, \
    /usr/bin/cat /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]-[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]/network.env, \
    /usr/sbin/vgs --noheadings -o vg_name, \
    /usr/sbin/vgs --noheadings -o vg_name atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings --nosuffix --units b --separator \, -o lv_name\,lv_size\,pool_lv\,origin atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -n * -o link show *, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -n * -o link show

# --- Capacity: the numbers placement reads on every provision --------------
# Live facts, not a bootstrap snapshot. Capacity that drifts silently is
# capacity that overcommits.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_HOST_CAPACITY = \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings --nosuffix --units b -o lv_size atlas/pool0, \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings -o data_percent atlas/pool0

# --- Liveness: is a Firecracker actually answering? ------------------------
# A socket inode outlives the process that bound it, so existence proves nothing
# and only a call through the socket separates live from stale. The probe is a
# GET of the API root, and it is deliberately a separate, fully literal line: the
# SendCtrlAltDel grant below must not be widened to cover it, because widening
# the one line that can halt a guest is how a read-only probe acquires the power
# to stop VMs. It is NOT `--fail`, which is the one flag the state-changing call
# wants and this one must not have — any HTTP answer, a 404 included, proves a
# live process, and reading a 404 as "not running" is how a controller decides a
# live VM is down.
#
# `ss` lives in /usr/bin on Ubuntu, not /usr/sbin, and sudoers matches the
# resolved path — so the wrong directory here was a silent denial. It was caught
# by probing every grant with `sudo -l` on a real host, and by nothing else.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_FIRECRACKER_LIVENESS = \
