# `systemctl kill -SIGKILL` would skip it and leave the source answering NDP for
# a /128). TimeoutStopSec is a load-time property, so the bound arrives as a
# runtime drop-in under /run and is removed again after the stop.
#
# The drop-in path carries the spelled-out UUID for the reason the lifecycle
# alias above gives: `firecracker-vm@*.service.d` also matched `sshd.service.d`,
# and a drop-in the daemon can write for an arbitrary unit, followed by the
# daemon-reload below and a start, is root.
#
# The `install` SOURCE is now a single literal path, and closing that was the
# highest-value change this file was waiting on. It used to be a wildcard:
# run.InstallFile spooled content through os.CreateTemp("", "boat-install-"), so
# the source was /tmp/boat-install-<random> and the sudoers line had to match it
# with `/tmp/boat-install-*`. The random tail is a variable-length decimal and no
# glob expresses "one or more digits", so the field was a `*` — and a `*` matches
# a space, so it could carry install's own `-t <directory>` flag and copy the
# daemon-written file into ANY directory: /etc/sudoers.d, a unit drop-in, root's
# .ssh. install(1) copies its first source before it errors on a missing tail, so
# the file landed even when the rest of the argv was junk. `sudo -u boat sudo -n
# id` afterwards returned uid=0. That was the whole boundary through one hole, and
# it was true of every `install` line in this file.
