# --- The Firecracker API socket --------------------------------------------
# The one `sh -c` on the list, and the reason it exists: the jailed socket's
# absolute path is longer than AF_UNIX's 108-byte sun_path allows, so the caller
# must cd into the socket's directory and address it by its short relative name —
# and that directory is 0700-owned by the per-VM uid, so the cd must be root.
# The cd and the curl are therefore one shell line by construction.
#
# It is pinned to the ONE call the stop path makes: SendCtrlAltDel, the
# cooperative power-off that lets the guest sync before the unit stops. Method,
# path, headers and body are all literal here. A trailing `*` instead would have
# been far easier and would have been a root shell — `*` matches spaces and
# semicolons in an argument, so anything appended to that pipeline would run.
#
# The UUID is spelled out for a subtler version of the same thing, and it took a
# real host to see it. The two path fields used to be `*`, on the reasoning that
# the literal tail (`/jail/firecracker/`, `/root/run && curl …`) did the pinning.
# It does not, because this is a SHELL: a `#` inside the wildcard comments the
# tail out, so the pattern still matches and the shell never runs it.
# `cd /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/x; chmod u+s /bin/sh; #/jail/…` satisfied
# this line. Inside an `sh -c`, a literal tail is not a pin, and every `sh -c`
# line in this file now spells its variable fields out.
#
# Two consequences, both intended:
#   - Later verbs (pause, resume, snapshot) each add their OWN pinned line for
#     their own method/path/body. Nobody widens this one.
#   - This line is byte-coupled to the template in internal/run's Firecracker
