# --- Parking: keeping a sleeping VM's address reachable --------------------
# A sleep runs the unit's ExecStopPost, which removes the proxy-NDP entry, the
# route, the namespace, the veth and the tap — that total teardown is what frees
# the RAM. These lines put back the least that lets one inbound SYN be noticed
# with no guest running: the host keeps answering NDP for the /128, and the /128
# routes out a shared always-up dummy so the packet is FORWARDED (and therefore
# seen by the forward chain) instead of being input-delivered to a host with
# nothing listening.
#
# `ip -6 route`, and never `ip netns exec`. The rule at the top of this file
# applies to every ip line here for the same reason it applies to the adoption
# one: `ip netns exec <ns> <cmd>` runs <cmd> as root with full access to the real
# filesystem, and no sudoers pattern can constrain it.
#
# `neigh del proxy` is granted beside `neigh replace proxy`, and the pairing is
# the point: a terminate has to withdraw the proxy-NDP entry a park installed, and
# it is the last moment anything can. A sleeping VM's unit is already inactive and
# `systemctl disable --now` does not re-run ExecStopPost, so the hook that would
# have unwound it never runs again — the host would go on answering neighbour
# solicitations for a /128 it no longer holds, and the day Atlas re-allocates that
# address the new VM inherits a stale entry pointing at nothing.
#
# `del` is granted in the SAME shape as `replace` rather than a tighter one,
# deliberately. It buys an attacker nothing `replace` did not already: both take
# one address and one device, both are bounded to the host's neighbour table, and
# a `replace` that can point another VM's /128 at this host is the same blast
# radius as a `del` that can remove it. Pinning one and not the other would be
# theatre. What tightened is BOTH, in code — see below.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK: the address and the uplink device are wildcards — they are read
# from a VM's network.env and from the host's default route, not derived from a
# UUID, so there is no shape to pin them to in sudoers. A canonical IPv6 has no
# fixed length (`::` to eight groups) and fnmatch has no "one or more of a class",
# so a character class cannot express one. Three things bound these lines.
#
# First, `ip` itself: the subcommand is fixed and positional, so the worst a
# wildcard buys is extra neighbour or route attributes on a command that is
# already installing or removing a neighbour entry and a route. It cannot become
# another ip subcommand, it cannot execute anything, and the route lines end in a
# literal `dev atlas-park0`.
#
# Second, internal/park validates the address with netip.ParseAddr and re-renders
# its canonical form before any of these render, so the value reaching `ip` is
# lowercase hex and colons and nothing else (park.canonicalAddress). That check
# now covers the UNDO as well as the install — park.removableAddress guards
# unpark's `route del` and retire's `neigh del proxy` — because a grant whose
# safety rests on a code-side check is worth exactly as much as the least-guarded
# path that reaches it, and the teardown paths read their address off disk.
#
# Third, what is NOT claimed: the uplink device stays a bare `*` with nothing
# after it on the two neigh lines. It is a kernel-supplied interface name from
# `ip -j -6 route show default`, not attacker input, and the tighter form — having
# park render the address in netip's fully expanded 39-character shape so this
# file could spell all eight groups out — was considered and declined. It would
# pin the address and still leave `dev *` unpinned on these two lines, while
# making the host's installed state spelled differently from the network.env that
# records it. The nft rule line has no trailing wildcard and is the one place that
# argument could still be worth revisiting.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_PARK_ROUTING = \
    /usr/sbin/ip link add atlas-park0 type dummy, \
    /usr/sbin/ip link set atlas-park0 up, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -6 neigh replace proxy * dev *, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -6 neigh del proxy * dev *, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -6 route replace */128 dev atlas-park0, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -6 route del */128 dev atlas-park0

# --- The wake trap: the nft counter and the rule that feeds it -------------
# Without these the trap polls nothing and wakes nothing, silently, forever. The
# poll runs `nft -j list counters` about once a second for the life of the host —
# a denial here does not fail an operation, it just means no sleeping VM on this
# host ever comes back from traffic. That is the failure this block exists to
# stop, and it is invisible from the control plane.
#
# The chain clause is one argument, spaces and all, which is why its spaces are
# escaped: internal/run.Quote wraps it in single quotes and internal/run.Split
# gives it back as a single argv entry. `policy accept` is what makes the trap
# TCP-only — a packet the wake rule does not match is forwarded out the dummy and
# dies there with nothing woken.
#
# nft parses `;` as a statement separator and `#` as a comment to end of line, and
# it re-lexes its whole argument vector to do so — which is why a value that
# run.Quote made one shell token still injects at the nft layer. Two fields here
# used to be wide open to that, and both are now closed as far as each shape
# allows:
#
#   - The COUNTER NAME is derived from a UUID (park.CounterName is wake_ + the 32
#     hex of the UUID with its dashes removed), so it has a fixed shape and is
#     spelled out: wake_ followed by 32 [0-9a-f]. On the three counter lines the
#     name is the trailing field, where a `wake_*` matched `wake_x; add rule …`
#     and was itself the injection point; spelling it out shuts that. On the rule
#     line it is defence in depth.
#
#   - The ADDRESS on the rule line is a VM's own /128, read from its network.env,
#     and has no fixed length to spell out — a canonical IPv6 runs from `::` to a
#     full eight groups and fnmatch has no "one or more of a class". It stays a
#     `*`, and what makes that safe is the first line of defence, now delivered:
#     internal/park validates the address with netip.ParseAddr and renders only
#     its canonical `.String()`, which is lowercase hex and colons and nothing
#     else — no space, no `;`, no `#` (park.canonicalAddress). The proven exploit
#     (`daddr 'fd00::1 drop; add counter …; #'`) satisfied this line because `*`
#     spanned the injection and the literal tail matched after the `#`; a value
#     that cannot contain `;` or `#` in the first place cannot do that.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK: this is still the loosest block in the file. If park's validation
# were bypassed, the `daddr *` would again let a crafted address reach nft, whose
# blast radius is the host's nftables ruleset — which includes the per-VM public
# firewall, so this is not nothing. nft has no form that executes a command and
# none that touches a file outside `-f`, so it stops at the ruleset. The rule
# handle on the delete line (`handle [0-9]*`) is nft's own output, numeric by
# construction; it is pinned to lead with a digit and not further.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_WAKE_TRAP = \
    /usr/sbin/nft list table inet atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/nft add table inet atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/nft list chain inet atlas forward, \
    /usr/sbin/nft -a list chain inet atlas forward, \
    /usr/sbin/nft add chain inet atlas forward {\ type\ filter\ hook\ forward\ priority\ filter;\ policy\ accept;\ }, \
    /usr/sbin/nft -j list counters table inet atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/nft list counter inet atlas wake_[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f], \
