# --- Volumes: the thin LVs a VM's disks are ---------------------------------
# Resize, rebuild and terminate are the three verbs that reach past the unit to
# the VM's LVM volumes. Naming is derived and never stored — atlas-vm-<uuid> is
# the root disk, atlas-data-<uuid> its peer — so a host that lost its database
# still knows which volume belongs to which VM, and that derivation is what these
# lines pin to.
#
# `lvs` is read-only and is allowed across the whole `atlas` group, because a
# rebuild's origin can be a base image or a snapshot LV and neither is named after
# the VM. Everything that WRITES is pinned to the two per-VM prefixes, so nothing
# here can reach pool0 or a base image — the shared state every VM on the host
# depends on. -K on lvchange is not optional: every per-VM disk is a thin snapshot
# and carries the activation-skip flag.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK: the trailing wildcards on lvcreate's `-n`, on lvextend and on
# blockdev could carry extra flags to those tools (a bigger `-L`, a different
# `--type`). Each of them takes a single volume operand and none of them removes
# anything, so the reachable damage is a wrongly sized or wrongly typed volume in
# a group this daemon may already create volumes in. Removal is the operation
# that cannot be undone, and it is in its own alias below with no wildcard at all.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_VOLUMES = \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings atlas/*, \
    /usr/sbin/blockdev --getsize64 /dev/atlas/atlas-vm-*, \
    /usr/sbin/blockdev --getsize64 /dev/atlas/atlas-data-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvcreate -s atlas/* -n atlas-vm-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvcreate -s atlas/* -n atlas-data-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvchange -ay -K atlas/atlas-vm-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvchange -ay -K atlas/atlas-data-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvextend -r -L [0-9]*G /dev/atlas/atlas-vm-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvextend -r -L [0-9]*G /dev/atlas/atlas-data-*, \
    /usr/sbin/lvextend -L [0-9]*G /dev/atlas/atlas-data-*, \
    /usr/sbin/vgmknodes atlas, \
    /usr/bin/udevadm settle

# --- Volume removal: the two lines that cannot be undone --------------------
# These two lines END with the UUID, with no literal text after it to do any
# pinning at all — the third of the three cases the header names. A `*` in a
