# --- Rebuild: writing a VM's identity into a fresh rootfs ------------------
# A rebuild lays down a new root filesystem from an image or a snapshot, and then
# has to make it THIS VM's rather than the source's: hostname, machine-id,
# authorized_keys, addresses, host keys, the data-disk fstab line. That happens
# with the disk mounted on the host, because the guest is not running — the one
# moment the host may mount a VM's root filesystem at all.
#
# EVERY path in this block is anchored on a mount point mktemp made, and the six
# characters mktemp generates are spelled out as [0-9A-Za-z] rather than
# wildcarded. This is the block where that mattered most, and it is worth being
# explicit about why. With a `*` or a `?` there, two things become true at once:
#
#   - `/tmp/atlas-mount-*/root/.ssh/authorized_keys` also matches
#     `/tmp/atlas-mount-x/../../root/.ssh/authorized_keys`, because a wildcard
#     matches `/` and `..` — so this block would hand out root's authorized_keys;
#   - the two `sh -c tee` lines would become a root shell, because a wildcard
#     matches `;` and a space, and the shell script's stdin is content this daemon
#     already controls. A trailing `*` on an `sh -c` line is arbitrary root
#     execution, and a `?` on one is arbitrary root execution six characters at a
#     time.
#
# A character class matches neither, so the mount point can only ever be a
# directory mktemp made from the one pinned template. The `tee -a` form is there
# because the redirection makes it a shell line; the path is substituted with the
# same quoting every other command's parameters get, so only the literal template
# is ever shell.
#
# Host keys are PRESERVED, not rotated: they are the VM's SSH identity and
# changing them on every rebuild looks exactly like an attack. The rm/ssh-keygen
# lines are the one exception — a disk with NO keys at all, where sshd would not
# start. Inherited rsa and ecdsa keys go because a snapshot source may carry them
# and keeping them would let the new VM answer with the source VM's identity.
#
# The FIXED identity files each keep their exact destination: authorized_keys,
# atlas-network.env, hostname and machine-id are pinned path and mode, and are
# defended better than the one line below them because Boat knows precisely where
# each lands.
#
# The `install -m 0644 <spool> <mount>/*` line is the exception, and it is the one
# open destination in this block on purpose: it lays down Identity.ExtraEnvironment
# — the arbitrary {path, content} guest files Atlas hands Boat to write and Boat
# writes without reading (the guest-service seam). Their paths are chosen by Atlas,
# so there is no fixed destination to pin; the `*` is the guest-absolute path,
# joined onto the mount point. Before this line existed the write was DENIED, and a
# rebuild fails after it has already dropped the root volume — so every bench VM's
# rebuild failed on the one command sudoers refused. What IS pinned: the source is
# the literal spool, the mode is 0644, and the mount-point suffix is six
# [0-9A-Za-z] mktemp made, so the file can only land under a directory mktemp
# created this rebuild.
#
# RESIDUAL RISK, shared by that install line and the `mkdir -p <mount>/*` for the
# data-disk mount point below it: a `*` matches `/` and `..`, so if the Go-side
# check were bypassed the guest path could be `../../etc/cron.d/x` and the write
# would land root-owned outside the mount. The first line of defence is in code and
# is symmetric now: vm.writeExtraEnvironment refuses a path that is not absolute or
# contains `..`, and vm.writeDataDiskMount makes the identical check on the mount
# point (internal/vm/identity.go). install writes one 0644 file and mkdir creates
# an empty directory — neither can execute — and every FIXED-destination write in
# this block is pinned, so this residual is one mislabelled file or one empty
# directory, not a foothold that compounds.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY = \
    /usr/bin/mktemp -d /tmp/atlas-mount-XXXXXX, \
    /usr/bin/mount /dev/atlas/atlas-vm-* /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z], \
    /usr/bin/umount /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z], \
    /usr/bin/rmdir /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z], \
    /usr/bin/install -d -m 0700 /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z]/root/.ssh, \
    /usr/bin/install -d -m 0755 /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z]/etc/ssh, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0600 /var/lib/boat/spool/install /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z]/root/.ssh/authorized_keys, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 /var/lib/boat/spool/install /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z]/etc/atlas-network.env, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 /var/lib/boat/spool/install /tmp/atlas-mount-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z]/etc/hostname, \
